  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1230 AM MST Fri Nov 20 2026\n\nIR currently depicts ample cloud coverage across the entire CWA\nwith cold cloud tops pushing around the upper level trough to\nthe east. This is where the strongest PVA anomalies are located\ncoming out of northeastern Utah into parts of Wyoming that will\ncontinue into Friday morning. The main finger of the PFJ\ncontinues to deepen the aforementioned trough with the 130kt jet\nmax pushing into its base near central California into southern\nNevada. This is allowing for the trough to dig further south\nwith more major impacts to be had for Colorado. Minor impacts\nstill expected for the CWA, mainly for western mountains with\ndecent accumulations. Radar shows the spreading of snow in from\nthe southwest and will continue for much of Friday as well. \n\nImmediate minor concerns will be for eastern Sweetwater to\nsouthern Natrona counties where ongoing light snow is making way\nfor slick roadways and reduced visibilities for the I-80 and Hwy\n287 corridors. This is mainly for points east of Rock Springs\nand Jeffrey City until at least mid Friday morning. A bit more\nbreezy as well for this area of the wind corridor that will help\nto aid in these impacts, especially that of the Friday morning\ncommute. Otherwise, western mountain snow continues overnight\nwith the highest snowfall rates not expected until the afternoon\nhours that will impact evening commutes over western passes to\ninclude Teton/Togwotee and extending now to South Pass that has\nbeen added to the winter weather advisory through late Friday\nnight. Pass level elevations will look at 2 to 5 inches of\naccumulations (60-80%), higher for western passes. Lower valleys\nare looking at 1 to 2 inches at best with lower confidence\n(20-30%). As the system becomes more open and progressive with\nits eastward progression, it will pivot to the east of the\nDivide that will extend mountain snow to the Bighorns dropping\nto parts of Natrona and Johnson Counties into Friday evening and\novernight lingering through Saturday morning. Bighorn mountain\npasses are looking at 2 to 4 inches of accumulation (30-40%) for\nFriday night and lower elevations to the east 1 to 2 inches.\nGusty winds will be the aid that could result in needed hazards\nlater on but holding off with BYZ to include northern Johnson\nalong with their Sheridan County. Otherwise, any snow into the\nWind River and Bighorn Basins will be limited in accumulation\nand for far eastern portions of these areas not expecting any\nimpacts at this time.\n\nThe main cold front will finally push through during Friday\nafternoon and into the evening allowing for a bit colder air to\nooze into the CWA for the weekend to be more seasonable to just\nbelow average for this time of year. Areas east of the Divide \nwill struggle to reach 40 degrees in the afternoon with \novernight lows down into the teens. The PFJ continues to remain \nsouth of the state through Monday to keep the colder \ntemperatures with minor chances for higher elevation snow at \ntimes during this time but limited in impact and mainly for \nareas like Yellowstone and higher western mountains. Mountain \npasses will stay mainly dry for the most part as the PFJ \nretreats north by Tuesday with induced ridging through late \nWednesday ahead of the next possible storm system Thanksgiving \nand beyond. It is looking more as an impact west of the Divide \nwith mountain snow and much lighter accumulation for the \nvalleys being a more zonal west flow off the Pacific. \nTemperatures not as cold more seasonable to just above average \nthrough the end of the week and into the following weekend. \nDetails unknown at this time but stay tuned for details in the \ncoming days.",
